<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="ch-power">
    <title>Power Management</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            The goal of this chapter is to reduce how much power an embedded system
            consumes when it has nothing to do.  Every program we have written this
            term keeps the processor running continuously, never idling, which is
            fine on USB power and wasteful on a battery.  The STM32C031C6 offers
            four low-power modes that stop parts of the chip when they are not
            needed, and one instruction, <c>__WFI()</c>, that parks the processor
            until an interrupt arrives.  We'll also introduce the independent
            watchdog, a hardware timer that resets the processor if the program
            stops making progress.
        </p>
        <p>
            This chapter covers one class day, the Wednesday x-hour of the final
            week.  In class we'll look at where a microcontroller's power goes, the
            four low-power modes and how a program enters one, and the independent
            watchdog.
        </p>
        <objectives>
            <ul>
                <li>Explain where a microcontroller's power goes: static leakage, and dynamic power proportional to the clock frequency.</li>
                <li>Name the STM32C031C6's four low-power modes, and state for each one what it stops and how long it takes to wake.</li>
                <li>Put the processor to sleep with <c>__WFI()</c> in an interrupt-driven main loop, and enter Stop mode with SLEEPDEEP.</li>
                <li>Explain what the independent watchdog does, and configure it with the key, prescaler and reload registers.</li>
            </ul>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-power-day17x">
        <title>Day 17x In-Class: Project Work, and Power Management</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Today we'll look at where a microcontroller's power goes, the four
                low-power modes and the one instruction that enters them, and the
                independent watchdog.
            </p>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day17x-session">
                <p>The unit below is optional, and this hour is above all project
                time; last year there was no time for sleep modes at all.  Running
                the full unit takes about 38 of the 50 minutes, which leaves
                roughly ten for project work, so the trade is the instructor's on
                the day.  The cut order is in the plan: the stretch in Part 4
                first, then Part 2's mode-entry detail, then Part 1 down to its
                one-liner and the ST figure.  The watchdog demonstration and its
                reveal are never cut.  Nothing in the final project depends on any
                of this, and the day has no reading and no homework.</p>
            </instructor>
        </introduction>

        <!-- 2 min agenda before Part 1; 2 + 6 + 10 + 20 + 12 = 50 -->
        <!-- Part 1: 6 min = 3 (where the power goes) + 3 (the levers, ST figure) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day17x-power">
            <title>Part 1: Where Does the Power Go?</title>
            <p>
                The microcontroller's circuits are made of transistors acting as
                switches (<xref ref="fig-transistor-switch-concept"/>).  Even when
                a transistor is off, a tiny current leaks through it from the
                supply to ground; that is <term>static power consumption</term>.
                To switch a transistor on or off, a tiny capacitance must be
                charged or discharged; that is <term>dynamic power
                consumption</term>, and it is proportional to the clock frequency,
                because every clock edge switches transistors.  For the Cortex-M0+
                core, the dynamic power ranges from about 5 to 50 µW per MHz.
            </p>
            <p>
                To reduce power consumption, enable only the internal peripherals you are actually using, and
                disable them when you are done, because a peripheral whose clock is
                stopped consumes almost nothing; you have been enabling peripheral
                clocks through <c>RCC-&gt;IOPENR</c> and its siblings all term, and
                the same registers turn them off.  Run the processor at the lowest
                clock speed that does the job.  Let the processor sleep when it has
                nothing to do, using the low-power modes below.  And read the
                datasheets of all the components you are using, and run tests at
                different clock frequencies, because in a whole system the
                microcontroller is rarely the only consumer.  For low power
                consumption, disable anything you don't need.  The deeper the
                sleep, the longer it can take to restore the system to its
                operating state after waking (<xref ref="fig-power-st-modes"/>).
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-power-st-modes">
                <caption>The STM32C0's operating modes, with typical current and,
                for the low-power modes, typical wake-up time, at a supply of 3 V
                and 25 °C.  Run mode at 48 MHz draws 58 µA per MHz.  Each mode
                below Run mode draws less and takes longer to wake from: Sleep at 16 MHz
                draws 20 µA per MHz and resumes in 10 clock cycles; Stop draws
                80 µA and wakes in 5.9 µs; Standby draws 7.45 µA and wakes in
                23 µs; Shutdown draws 19 nA, a thousand times less than a
                microamp, and takes 385 µs.  Figure from
                STMicroelectronics.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day17x-Sleep_Modes/slide06_img1.png" width="80%"/>
            </figure>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 2: 10 min = 2 (clock tree) + 4 (the four modes, Table 19) + 2 (the worked Stop entry) + 2 (the loop) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day17x-modes">
            <title>Part 2: The Four Low-Power Modes, and How You Enter One</title>
            <p>
                The STM32C031C6 contains many separate <term>clock domains</term>,
                each serving one part of the chip
                (<xref ref="fig-power-clock-tree"/>).  Stopping a clock disables the
                corresponding domain and saves its power.  The clock we have used
                all term is the HSI48, a 48 MHz internal oscillator whose output is
                divided by four at reset to give the 12 MHz system clock.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-power-clock-tree">
                <caption>Clock distribution in the STM32C0, Figure 9 of the
                Reference Manual.  The HSI48 oscillator at the left feeds the
                system clock through the HSIDIV divider, whose output HSISYS is
                the clock the CPU runs on; HSIDIV divides by four at reset, which
                is where our 12 MHz comes from.  Other oscillators, including the
                32 kHz low-speed internal oscillator (LSI) that clocks the
                watchdog, serve their own domains.  Stopping a clock stops the
                domain it feeds, and saves its power.  The blue path is our
                clock; the boxes at the right are the domains it
                serves.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day17x-Sleep_Modes/fig-clock-tree-annotated.svg" width="85%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                The low-power modes stop progressively more of the chip.  The
                device transitions from Run mode into a low-power mode and back;
                there is no path from one low-power mode to another
                (<xref ref="fig-power-transit"/>).  The Reference Manual's Table
                19, <em>Device resources enabled in different operating
                modes</em>, lists exactly what runs and what can wake the device
                in each mode (<xref ref="fig-power-resources"/>); the four rows
                below are its summary.
            </p>
            <table xml:id="table-day17x-modes">
                <title>The four low-power modes</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor" right="minor">
                <col right="minor"/>
                <col right="minor"/>
                <col right="minor"/>
                <col right="minor"/>
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Mode</cell>
                    <cell>What stops</cell>
                    <cell>What survives</cell>
                    <cell>What wakes it</cell>
                </row>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><term>Sleep</term></cell>
                    <cell>the CPU clock</cell>
                    <cell>all peripherals can run and wake up the CPU; peripheral clocks can be disabled before going to sleep</cell>
                    <cell>any enabled interrupt, in a few clock cycles: every peripheral keeps its clock, so no special wakeup capability is needed</cell>
                </row>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><term>Stop</term></cell>
                    <cell>the CPU clock and the high-speed oscillators</cell>
                    <cell>SRAM and register contents; the 32 kHz low-speed oscillator (LSI) that clocks the watchdog, and a few wakeup-capable peripherals; GPIO pin states</cell>
                    <cell>an interrupt from a peripheral that still has a clock</cell>
                </row>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><term>Standby</term></cell>
                    <cell>the core's supply domain, the part of the chip that powers the CPU and most peripherals</cell>
                    <cell>SRAM and register contents are lost, except PWR control register 3; the IWDG and LSI can keep running</cell>
                    <cell>the reset pin or one of the dedicated wakeup (WKUP) pins; Reference Manual Table 19 lists the rest</cell>
                </row>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><term>Shutdown</term></cell>
                    <cell>all oscillators and the supply monitor</cell>
                    <cell>nothing; contents are lost, and waking behaves like a reset</cell>
                    <cell>the reset pin or a wakeup (WKUP) pin</cell>
                </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <figure xml:id="fig-power-transit">
                <caption>The low-power mode transit diagram, Figure 7 of the
                Reference Manual.  Every low-power mode is entered from Run mode
                and exits back to Run mode.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day17x-Sleep_Modes/slide08_img1.png" width="100%"/>
            </figure>
            <figure xml:id="fig-power-resources">
                <caption>Reference Manual Table 19: which resources run in each
                operating mode, and which can wake the device.  In ST's notation
                Y is enabled, O optional (off by default, software can enable
                it), U unchanged from before entry, A automatically disabled, and
                a dash not available; the gray column beside Stop, Standby and
                Shutdown is the resource's ability to wake the device.  Reading
                down the columns, Sleep keeps everything but the CPU available;
                Stop keeps SRAM, GPIO pin states, the LSI and a few
                wakeup-capable peripherals; Standby and Shutdown keep almost
                nothing.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day17x-Sleep_Modes/slide09_img1.png" width="100%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                To put the processor into a low-power mode, you set two fields
                and execute one instruction.  Set the LPMS (low-power mode
                selection) field in <c>PWR_CR1</c> to the desired mode
                (<xref ref="fig-pwr-cr1"/>).  Set the SLEEPDEEP bit in the
                Cortex-M0+ system control register, <c>SCB-&gt;SCR</c>
                (documented in the Arm Cortex-M0+ Generic User Guide, not in
                RM0490).  Then execute <c>__WFI()</c> (wait for interrupt).
                Plain Sleep mode needs no register
                writes at all: with SLEEPDEEP clear, <c>__WFI()</c>
                stops the CPU clock until an interrupt
                arrives.  For Stop mode, LPMS is 000 and SLEEPDEEP is set;
                <xref ref="fig-power-entry"/> lays out the full table.  The PWR
                peripheral's registers,
                like every peripheral's, are reachable only while its clock is
                enabled, so the sequence is:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
    RCC->APBENR1 |= RCC_APBENR1_PWREN;    // clock the PWR peripheral
    PWR->CR1 &= ~PWR_CR1_LPMS_Msk;        // LPMS = 000 selects Stop mode
    SCB->SCR |= SCB_SCR_SLEEPDEEP_Msk;    // deep sleep rather than Sleep
    __WFI();                              // enter Stop mode; an interrupt wakes us
]]></code></program>
            <figure xml:id="fig-pwr-cr1">
                <caption>The low half of the PWR_CR1 register (Reference Manual
                §4.4.1), with the LPMS field circled: bits 2 to 0 select the
                low-power mode entered when SLEEPDEEP is set, and 000 is Stop
                mode.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day17x-Sleep_Modes/fig-pwr-cr1-circled.png" width="100%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Waking runs the entry backwards.  An appropriate interrupt
                occurs, the HSI48 restarts, the interrupt's own handler runs, and
                then execution resumes at the instruction after <c>__WFI()</c>;
                the program clears SLEEPDEEP if the next sleep should be a plain
                one.  The deeper modes, Standby and Shutdown, are selected
                through the same LPMS field; their entry values are in
                <xref ref="sec-power-reference"/>.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-power-entry">
                <caption>Reference Manual Table 20, trimmed to the columns we
                use: the low-power mode entered for each combination of
                SLEEPDEEP, LPMS and entry instruction.  Sleep needs only
                SLEEPDEEP clear; Stop is SLEEPDEEP set with LPMS at 000.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day17x-Sleep_Modes/fig-power-entry-trim.png" width="70%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                The place where <c>__WFI()</c> belongs in a program is the main
                loop of an interrupt-driven design.  Initialize the devices, enable the interrupts, and
                then loop: check the flags your ISRs set, take whatever action they
                call for, and go to sleep until the next interrupt.  At least one
                enabled interrupt must exist, or nothing will ever wake the
                processor.
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
int main(void) {
    // Initialize devices, enable interrupts
    ...
    while (1) {
        // Check interrupt flags, take action
        ...
        // Go to sleep, await the next interrupt
        __WFI();
        // When the CPU wakes up, execution resumes here and the loop repeats
    }
    return 1;
}
// ISRs (including at least one interrupt that awakens the processor!)
...
]]></code></program>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 3: 20 min = 3 (what it does) + 12 (the quick example, both conditions) + 2 (the reveal) + 3 (the configuration) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day17x-watchdog">
            <title>Part 3: The Watchdog</title>
            <p>
                A deployed embedded system has nobody watching it.  If the program
                gets stuck, in a loop that never exits or waiting for an event that
                never occurs, the system stops responding, and on a shelf or
                a rooftop there is no one to press the reset button.  The
                <term>independent watchdog</term> (IWDG) is the hardware answer.
                It is a 12-bit downcounter with an 8-bit prescaler, running on its
                own 32 kHz internal clock, the LSI, separate from the clock that
                runs the processor.  That is what independent means here: the
                watchdog keeps counting even if the main clock stops.  A healthy
                program reloads the counter often, which is called
                <term>petting the dog</term>.  If the program gets stuck and the
                reload stops coming, the counter reaches zero and the watchdog
                resets the processor.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-watchdog-dog">
                <caption>The watchdog at rest.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day17x-Sleep_Modes/fig-watchdog-dog.png" width="55%"/>
            </figure>
            <activity xml:id="act-day17x-watchdog">
                <title>A quick example</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Make a copy of <c>TemplateProject</c>, call the copy
                    <c>Watchdog</c>, and copy <c>watchdog.c</c> from Canvas into
                    the new project's <c>Src</c> folder.  Compile, upload to your
                    Nucleo, and open CoolTerm at 9600 baud.  You'll see a
                    <c>Hello dog world</c> message that seems to repeat every
                    4 seconds.</p>
                </introduction>
                <!-- No predict task here: her slides 17-18 are do-then-reveal,
                     and B-19 forbids adding one. -->
                <task xml:id="task-day17x-press-often">
                    <statement><p>Press the blue user button repeatedly, more
                    frequently than every 4 seconds, and observe what
                    happens.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day17x-press-rarely">
                    <statement><p>Now press it less frequently than every 4 seconds,
                    and observe the difference.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                What actually happens: the <c>Hello dog world</c> message is
                printed at the very start of the program, before the event loop, so
                seeing the message over and over means the device is rebooting
                regularly.  When you press the blue user button, the program is
                petting the watchdog, thereby resetting the countdown that would
                otherwise have ended in a reboot.  If you pet the watchdog
                frequently enough, the program continues running without
                rebooting.
            </p>
            <p>
                The configuration steps are visible in the listing, and each one is
                a write to one of the watchdog's registers
                (<url href="external/stm32c031_rm.pdf">Reference Manual</url>
                §20).  Writing 0x0000 5555 to the key register <c>IWDG_KR</c>
                unlocks the protected prescaler and reload registers.  The
                prescaler <c>IWDG_PR</c> sets the counting rate; 0b011 divides the
                32 kHz LSI by 32, giving a 1 ms tick.  The watchdog needs a
                moment to absorb each of these writes, and that is what the two
                <c>while</c> loops in the listing wait for.
                Writing 0x0000 CCCC to <c>IWDG_KR</c> starts the watchdog.
                Writing 0x0000 AAAA to <c>IWDG_KR</c> reloads the counter to the value in
                <c>IWDG_RLR</c>, 4000 in the listing, and that is what petting
                the dog does in the program: it restarts the 4 second countdown
                before it can reach zero.
            </p>
            <p>
                The blue user button is on PC13, which has its own pull-up
                resistor built in.
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include "ES28.h"
#include "uart.h"
#include <stdio.h>
// A few magic numbers for the watchdog (from the datasheet)
#define KEY_ACCESS 0x00005555
#define KEY_UPDATE 0x0000AAAA
#define KEY_START  0x0000CCCC
int main( void ) {
    uart2_init();
    printf( "Hello dog world\r\n");
    // Use the built-in blue user button as a proxy for petting the dog.
    RCC->IOPENR |= RCC_IOPENR_GPIOCEN;
    GPIOC->MODER = (GPIOC->MODER & ~GPIO_MODER_MODE13_Msk) | (GPIO_INPUT << GPIO_MODER_MODE13_Pos);
    // The watchdog is always accessible, so no need to turn any clocks on for it.
    // First, unlock the watchdog registers by writing a special value to the key register
    IWDG->KR = KEY_ACCESS;
    // Set up the prescaler - the watchdog clock runs at 32 kHz.  We will set up a 1 ms tick.
    while( IWDG->SR & IWDG_SR_PVU ); // Can only update when the PVU status bit is clear.
    IWDG->PR = 0b011;
    // Reload register is set to 4000: we need to pet the dog at least once every 4 secs
    while( IWDG->SR & IWDG_SR_RVU ); // Can only update when the RVU status bit is clear
    IWDG->RLR = 4000;
    // Wake the dog...
    IWDG->KR = KEY_START;
    // Now, loop and pet the dog whenever the blue user button is pushed.
    while(1){
        if ( (GPIOC->IDR & GPIO_IDR_ID13) == 0 ){
            // Pet the dog
            IWDG->KR = KEY_UPDATE;
            printf( "Nice Doggy\r\n");
            delay_ms(100);  // be nice to the terminal.
        }
    }
    return 1;
}
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                The IWDG also has a window option, which generates a reset if the
                dog is petted too early as well as too late.  The STM32C031C6
                carries a second watchdog, the system window watchdog (WWDG),
                which runs on the main clock and can generate an early interrupt
                before it resets the processor; it is described in Reference
                Manual §21.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 4: 12 min. The stretch is briefed in a sentence and done inside
             the work time; if the hour is short it is the first thing cut
             (plans/day17x.md cut order). -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day17x-stretch">
            <title>Part 4: A Super Quick Modification, then Project Time</title>
            <activity xml:id="act-day17x-needier">
                <title>Super quick modification</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day17x-onesec">
                    <statement><p>Make the dog even needier: have it need a pet
                    every 1 second.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day17x-limit">
                    <statement><p>Make it needier still.  Add a <c>printf()</c>
                    statement after the watchdog initialization but before the
                    <c>while</c> loop, so it runs before you get a chance to pet
                    the dog.  How needy can you make it before it resets too fast
                    to even print your message?  Push the reload value down until
                    the message starts arriving garbled or not at all; a partial
                    line repeating in CoolTerm is what that looks like, and it is
                    the expected result rather than a fault in your
                    program.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day17x-needier">
                <p>The 1 second dog is <c>IWDG-&gt;RLR = 1000;</c> with the same
                prescaler.  For the limit: at 9600 baud a character takes about
                1.04 ms (ten bit times per frame), so a 17-character message such
                as <c>Hello dog world\r\n</c> takes about 18 ms to leave the
                USART.  A reload value much below about 20 resets the chip in the
                middle of the message, and the terminal shows a partial line
                repeating.  Students will find the edge is not sharp, because the
                reset and restart add time of their own.  With the countdown
                starting from 0xFFF, the edge only appears after the first pet,
                so a student should press the button once before pushing the
                reload value down.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-power-reference">
        <title>Reference: The Low-Power Modes and the Watchdog</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                This section collects the four modes and the IWDG's registers for
                look-up, including the details class did not cover: what the
                watchdog's counter does before its first reload, the prescaler's
                full range, and what survives in each mode.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-power-ref-modes">
            <title>The Four Modes, and Their Entry Values</title>
            <p>
                The four modes are summarized in
                <xref ref="table-day17x-modes"/>; Reference Manual §4.3 and its
                Table 19 carry the full accounting, and the details worth having
                when you come back to this are the following.  Sleep stops the
                CPU clock only.  Stop also stops the high-speed oscillators, the
                HSI48 and the HSE (the high-speed external oscillator); SRAM and
                register contents are retained, the LSI and the LSE (the
                low-speed internal and external oscillators) can keep running,
                and exiting re-enables the HSI48 with the system clock divider
                left at whatever it was set to before entry.  Standby powers off
                the core's supply domain: SRAM and register contents are lost and
                <c>PWR_CR1</c> is reset to its default, while <c>PWR_CR3</c>,
                <c>PWR_CR4</c> and the backup registers are retained and the IWDG
                and LSI can keep running.  Shutdown disables all oscillators and
                the supply monitor; contents are lost, and exiting behaves like a
                reset.
            </p>
            <p>
                Entry is controlled by SLEEPDEEP (in the Cortex-M0+ system control
                register, <c>SCB-&gt;SCR</c>) and LPMS[2:0] (in <c>PWR_CR1</c>,
                <xref ref="fig-pwr-cr1"/>), then executing <c>__WFI()</c> or
                <c>__WFE()</c> (Reference Manual §4.3.2, Table 20):
            </p>
            <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Mode</cell>
                    <cell>SLEEPDEEP</cell>
                    <cell>LPMS[2:0]</cell>
                </row>
                <row><cell>Sleep</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>any</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Stop</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>000 (the reset value)</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Standby</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>011</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Shutdown</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>1XX</cell></row>
            </tabular>
            <p>
                Waking from Sleep or Stop resumes execution after the
                <c>__WFI()</c>; the pending flag of the interrupt that caused the
                wake must be cleared as usual.  Waking from Standby or Shutdown
                restarts the program from the beginning, as after a reset.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-power-ref-iwdg">
            <title>Configuring the IWDG</title>
            <p>
                The independent watchdog has five registers (Reference Manual
                §20.4), four of which this course uses: the key register
                <c>IWDG_KR</c>, the prescaler <c>IWDG_PR</c>, the reload register
                <c>IWDG_RLR</c> and the status register <c>IWDG_SR</c>.  The
                fifth, <c>IWDG_WINR</c>, sets the window option we do not use.
                All key values are written to <c>IWDG_KR</c>: 0x0000 5555 unlocks
                the prescaler, reload and window registers; 0x0000 CCCC starts
                the watchdog, whose counter counts down from 0xFFF; 0x0000 AAAA
                reloads the counter from <c>IWDG_RLR</c> and prevents the reset.
                The prescaler <c>IWDG_PR</c> divides the 32 kHz LSI: 000 is
                divide by 4, each step doubles it, 011 is divide by 32 (a 1 ms
                tick), and 110 and 111 are divide by 256.  The timeout is the
                reload value times the prescaled tick.  The IWDG needs no clock
                enable of its own, unlike every other peripheral this term,
                because it is clocked by the LSI rather than from the bus that
                RCC gates.
            </p>
            <p>
                Once started, the IWDG cannot be turned off by software: there is
                no disable bit, which is the point of a watchdog.  This is not a
                permanent change to the chip.  The IWDG only runs at all once a
                program has written 0x0000 CCCC to <c>IWDG_KR</c>, and any reset,
                the watchdog's own, the board's reset button, or flashing a new
                program, starts the chip with the watchdog stopped.  (Reference
                Manual §20.3.3 describes a hardware-watchdog option byte that
                starts the IWDG automatically at power-up; this course does not
                set it.)  It keeps counting through Sleep, Stop and Standby, so a
                program that sleeps for longer than its watchdog timeout is reset
                before it wakes.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>
</chapter>
